= La comida

The invitation is handwritten, which is the tell: a Secretario de Estado would like the pleasure of the parliamentary leader's company, informally, no agenda, no press, at a restaurant discreet enough to have no sign. This is not negotiation. This is *cultivo* — cultivation, the regime's most refined instrument, the long lunch across which a generation of oppositionists have been, over excellent wine and genuine charm, quietly converted from enemies into interlocutors who can be called at home.

The Secretario is, infuriatingly, good company: well-read, self-deprecating about the regime's sins, apparently sympathetic, careful to concede your best points. By dessert you will either have a private channel to power worth more than ten speeches — or the first thread of a web it takes years to notice you are inside.

- @comida_usar: Take the channel and use it coldly — extract what access gives, concede nothing, keep the receipts.
- @comida_publicar: Neutralize the cultivation by publicizing it — report the lunch and its overtures to your own caucus, on the record.
- @comida_declinar: Decline the intimacy. Meet only formally, in rooms with minutes; refuse to be someone the Secretario can call at home.

@comida_usar
choose-if: unity >= 45
unavailable-subtitle: Playing the access game without being swallowed by it needs a caucus disciplined enough to trust — yours is fractious.
on-arrival: inteligencia += 4; political_capital += 4; funds += 2; absorcion += 6; pri_reformist_rel += 3

You take the channel and resolve to be the one doing the using: the private line delivers real intelligence on the regime's fractures and the occasional genuine concession pried loose off the record, and you give up nothing the base could not read in the paper. It works, mostly, and it is exactly how everyone who was ever cultivated described the early months — the mutual respect, the useful exchanges, the certainty of being the cook and not the meal. The web is patient. You are, for now, ahead of it.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@comida_publicar
on-arrival: absorcion -= 6; sup_students += 2; unity += 2; pri_reformist_rel -= 4; legitimidad_regimen -= 1

You break the spell the only way it can be broken — by refusing the privacy that is the whole mechanism: the lunch and its soft overtures are reported to your own caucus and, in outline, beyond it, so that the cultivation becomes a public fact instead of a private thread. The Secretario is embarrassed and the channel closes and the antechambers mark you as someone who cannot be dined, which is the point. Access was the bait; transparency was the hook you declined to swallow.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@comida_declinar
on-arrival: absorcion -= 4; pri_reformist_rel -= 3; unity += 1; political_capital -= 1

You send regrets and a counter-proposal: any matter the Secretario wishes to raise can be raised in a meeting with minutes and a witness. It is a small rudeness with a large meaning — you will not be a man the regime can call at home — and it costs you the channel and the concessions the channel might have carried. The founders would approve; the pragmatists in the caucus wonder aloud how many campesinos went unhelped to protect the purity of your dinner reservations.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@el_voto_dificil
title: El voto difícil
new-page: true
subtitle: The bill that buys something and costs everything
face-image: img/pcmconf.jpg
tags: event
view-if: via = "institucional" and izq_seats >= 2 and time >= voto_inst_last + 20 and year >= 1980
priority: 3
frequency: 55
max-visits: 4
on-arrival: voto_inst_last = time

= El voto difícil

The bill is a poisoned gift and it was aimed at you specifically. It contains something the Party genuinely wants — a real amnesty clause, a labor protection, a budget line for the campo — bolted onto something it cannot stomach, a devaluation-enabling fine print or a security provision with teeth. The PRI has the votes to pass it without you. What it wants is your vote *on* it, because your yes launders the ugly half and your no forfeits the good half. The whip counts have already begun; the reformist caucus is split down the middle in the corridor.

- @voto_a_favor: Vote yes. Take the concrete good — the amnesty, the protection — and wear the compromise it came wrapped in.
- @voto_en_contra: Vote no. Refuse to launder the ugly half, and lose the good half with it, cleanly.
- @voto_abstencion: Abstain as a bloc, loudly — a public refusal to play the yes/no game the bill was built to trap you in.

@voto_a_favor
on-arrival: funds += 3; sup_workers += 2; sup_peasants += 2; absorcion += 7; fe_voto -= 2; sup_students -= 3; pri_reformist_rel += 4

The amnesty clause becomes law and men come home because of it; that is real and no sneer erases it. So does the fine print you voted for, and the PRI's press is delighted to report that the left endorsed the government's package — your yes, clipped from its context, running under a photograph of a handshake. You bought something concrete and paid in the coin the road always demands: a little more of the distance between you and the street, a little more of the base's suspicion that inside is where principles go to be traded by the clause.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@voto_en_contra
on-arrival: sup_students += 3; unity += 2; absorcion -= 6; sup_workers -= 1; pri_reformist_rel -= 4

You vote no and keep your hands clean and the amnesty dies with the fine print, which means specific men do not come home who might have — a purity with a name and a face in a cell. The base cheers the principle; the families of the un-amnestied do not. It is the hardest arithmetic on the reformist road, the one they do not warn you about: that inside the machine, sometimes the clean vote is the one that leaves someone in prison. You chose not to be the regime's launderer. Someone else paid for the choice.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@voto_abstencion
on-arrival: legitimidad_regimen -= 2; sup_students += 1; political_capital += 2; pri_reformist_rel -= 1; unity -= 1

The caucus abstains as one and reads a statement into the record explaining exactly why — that the bill was engineered to make principle impossible and the Party refuses to certify the engineering. It is the sophisticated move and it satisfies no one fully: the good half still dies, the ugly half still passes, and the country's headline is the ambiguous *la izquierda se abstiene,* which the regime spins and the base finds hard to celebrate. You refused the frame and kept a shred of both principle and the concession's memory. On this road, some days, refusing the question is the most honest answer available.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@nos_vendieron
title: "Nos vendieron"
new-page: true
subtitle: The street accuses the caucus
face-image: img/pcmconf.jpg
tags: event
view-if: via = "institucional" and absorcion >= 40 and time >= vendieron_last + 20
priority: 3
frequency: 60
max-visits: 3
on-arrival: vendieron_last = time

= "Nos vendieron"

It surfaces at a coordinadora assembly and travels fast because it has been waiting to be said: that the parliamentary group has gone native, that the deputies eat better than the base, that the Party that was built in the plazas now lives in the antechambers and votes like it. *Nos vendieron* — they sold us. The word is unfair and it is not wrong, and the leadership can feel the difference between the two shrinking. The street current and the parliamentary current are, for the first time, addressing each other as adversaries.

This is the reformist road's civil war, the one every entrist party eventually fights: the people who won the seats versus the people who did the organizing that made the seats winnable, each certain the other has forgotten the point.

- @vendieron_autocritica: Call it — a public autocrítica, the caucus submits to the base, discipline reasserted from below.
- @vendieron_defender: Defend the deputies — the seats are real power honestly won, and the street's purism would throw it away.
- @vendieron_puente: Build the bridge — bind the caucus to the movement by rule: mandated plaza time, seats accountable to assemblies.

@vendieron_autocritica
on-arrival: absorcion -= 12; unity += 3; sup_students += 3; pri_reformist_rel -= 4; political_capital -= 3

The deputies stand before the assembly and take it — the criticism, the accounting, the reminder of what they were sent inside to do — and something old and healthy in the Party's culture reasserts itself: the cadre is accountable to the base, even the cadre with a laminated credential. It costs the caucus standing in the antechambers, where public self-flagellation reads as weakness, and it costs some proud deputies their pride. It buys back the thing absorption was quietly stealing: the base's belief that the seats are still theirs.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@vendieron_defender
on-arrival: absorcion += 6; pri_reformist_rel += 2; funds += 1; sup_students -= 4; unity -= 4; guerrilla_meter += 2

The leadership sides with its deputies: the seats are real, the power is real, the amendments and the tribune and the committee reports are worth more than the plaza's applause, and the street's purism is a luxury of people who never had to count votes. It is a defensible argument and it widens the crack into a canyon — the coordinadoras conclude the Party has chosen its functionaries over its founders, some of the youngest and angriest stop coming to anything with the Party's name on it, and absorption, defended, deepens. You kept the caucus and strained the movement that feeds it.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@vendieron_puente
choose-if: unity >= 42
unavailable-subtitle: Binding two currents by rule takes a party unified enough to accept the rule — yours is too frayed.
on-arrival: absorcion -= 8; unity += 4; frente_unidad += 2; sup_students += 2; political_capital += 2

You refuse the false choice and legislate the relationship instead: deputies mandated to spend real time in the colonias and the picket lines, caucus positions accountable to the assemblies, the seats formally tethered to the movement that won them. It is bureaucratic and it is exactly right — it makes absorption structurally harder by keeping the antechamber and the plaza in the same body, arguing, on purpose, forever. The street keeps its deputies on a leash it controls; the deputies keep their power; the Party keeps, for now, the hardest thing on this road to keep — itself.

- @post_event: Continuar.
